## Core types + helpers (`src/i18n/index.ts`)

```ts
export const LOCALES = ["ar", "en", "fr"] as const;
export type Locale = typeof LOCALES[number];
export const DEFAULT_LOCALE: Locale = "ar";

export const LOCALE_META: Record<Locale, {
  name: string; flag: string; dir: "rtl" | "ltr";
}> = {
  ar: { name: "العربية",  flag: "🇸🇦", dir: "rtl" },
  en: { name: "English",   flag: "🇬🇧", dir: "ltr" },
  fr: { name: "Français",  flag: "🇫🇷", dir: "ltr" },
};

export function isLocale(v: unknown): v is Locale {
  return typeof v === "string" && (LOCALES as readonly string[]).includes(v);
}

export function dirFor(loc: Locale): "rtl" | "ltr" {
  return LOCALE_META[loc].dir;
}

// IMPORTANT: type alias MUST come before DICTS — TS strict mode
// rejects `Record<string, string>` used as an index type otherwise.
type Dict = Record<string, string>;
export const DICTS: Record<Locale, Dict> = {
  ar: DICTS_AR,
  en: DICTS_EN,
  fr: DICTS_FR,
};

export function t(locale: Locale, key: string, vars?: Record<string, string | number>): string {
  const raw = DICTS[locale][key] ?? DICTS[DEFAULT_LOCALE][key] ?? key;
  if (!vars) return raw;
  return raw.replace(/\{(\w+)\}/g, (_, k) => String(vars[k] ?? `{${k}}`));
}

export function makeT(locale: Locale) {
  return (key: string, vars?: Record<string, string | number>) => t(locale, key, vars);
}
```

**Pitfall #34 — type ordering:** `type Dict` MUST be declared BEFORE
`const DICTS: Record<Locale, Dict>`. TS strict mode rejects
`Record<string, string>` used as a Record index type if the alias
is declared after. Symptom: `error TS2456: Type 'Dict' has no
properties in common with type 'Record<Locale, ...>'`.

**Pitfall #35 — Dict type at all:** an inline `Record<string, string>`
type DOES work but loses IntelliSense on the dict object's keys.
The named alias gives you hover-jump to the dict file from any
`t("nav.dashboard")` call.

**Pitfall #36 — duplicate keys in `const DICTS_AR = { ... }`:**
TS lets you write `"nav.foo": "x", "nav.foo": "y"` in a single
object literal, but the second value wins at runtime AND tsc
strict-mode emits `error TS1117: An object literal cannot have
multiple properties with the same name`. Build still succeeds
because `next build` uses its own checker, but the route that
imports the dict throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of
undefined` when the bundle optimizes the duplicate property away
unexpectedly. Always `grep -nE '"nav\.' src/i18n/dictionaries/ar.ts`
before adding new keys to verify no collision.

**Pitfall #49 — `patch` tool breaks object literal indentation on nested blocks:**
when you `patch` a dict file to add a block of 5-10 new keys, the
`new_string` indentation is interpreted relative to `old_string`'s
leading whitespace, NOT relative to the surrounding object literal
scope. Result: lines 64-76 of `ar.ts` ended up with 4-space indent
inside a 2-space object literal, and the parser silently accepted
the file but the runtime evaluation of `DICTS_AR[key]` returned
`undefined` for the mis-indented keys (because the JS engine treats
them as a new block scope). Symptom: `TypeError: undefined is not
an object (evaluating 'originalFactory.call')` on routes that
import the dict — Next's RSC bundle can't resolve the dict lookup
and the React factory chain breaks at hydration. Fix: when adding
blocks of keys, **read the FULL dict file first**, then `write_file`
with the complete corrected content. Never patch more than 2-3 keys
into a dict block at a time.

**Pitfall #50 — `patch` can introduce a premature `};` close:**
when `old_string` ends with `\n` (newline) and the line after it is
already `};`, the `new_string` insertion lands ABOVE the existing
close. If the surrounding context looks "normal" to the patcher's
fuzzy match, the diff lands cleanly, but the file now has an extra
`};` mid-file. Symptom: `error TS1117: An object literal cannot have
multiple properties with the same name` (because the orphan close
turns everything after it into a separate block). Always check the
total count of `^};` lines before and after a patch:
`grep -c '^};' file.ts` — should match the expected number of object
literals in the file.

**Pitfall #51 — `patch` adding new keys to a block that already has them:**
classic extension-pitfall. If you `patch` "add `platform.qiwa` to the
sidebar block" but the file already has `platform.qiwa` from an
earlier (perhaps reverted) patch, you now have TWO
`"platform.qiwa": "..."` keys. JS keeps the last one — but tsc strict
mode emits `TS1117`. Worse, when the block ALSO has a duplicate
*value key* like `common.user`, runtime dict lookup is unreliable.
Always `grep -nE '"<key>":' file.ts | wc -l` before patching a new
key; if it's >1, you have a duplicate.

