## Client hook — context-based (post-fix, zero flash)

```tsx
// src/i18n/I18nProvider.tsx
"use client";
import { createContext, useContext, useMemo, type ReactNode } from "react";
import { makeT, DEFAULT_LOCALE, dirFor, LOCALE_META, type Locale } from "./index";

export interface I18nContextValue {
  locale: Locale;
  dir: "rtl" | "ltr";
  meta: (typeof LOCALE_META)[Locale];
  t: (key: string, vars?: Record<string, string | number>) => string;
}

const I18nContext = createContext<I18nContextValue | null>(null);

export function I18nProvider({ locale, children }: { locale: Locale; children: ReactNode }) {
  const value = useMemo<I18nContextValue>(() => {
    const t = makeT(locale);
    return { locale, dir: dirFor(locale), meta: LOCALE_META[locale], t };
  }, [locale]);
  return <I18nContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</I18nContext.Provider>;
}

export function useT(): I18nContextValue {
  const ctx = useContext(I18nContext);
  if (!ctx) {
    // Fallback for components rendered outside the provider (error boundaries)
    const t = makeT(DEFAULT_LOCALE);
    return { locale: DEFAULT_LOCALE, dir: dirFor(DEFAULT_LOCALE), meta: LOCALE_META[DEFAULT_LOCALE], t };
  }
  return ctx;
}

export function useLocale(): Locale { return useT().locale; }
export function useDir(): "rtl" | "ltr" { return useT().dir; }
```

```tsx
// src/i18n/useT.tsx (re-export shim so old imports still work)
export { I18nProvider, useT, useLocale, useDir } from "./I18nProvider";
```

```tsx
// src/app/layout.tsx — wrap with I18nProvider so client components read locale from context
import { getLocale, dirFor } from "@/i18n/server";
import { I18nProvider } from "@/i18n/useT";

export default async function RootLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const locale = await getLocale();
  const dir = dirFor(locale);
  return (
    <html lang={locale} dir={dir}>
      <body>
        <I18nProvider locale={locale}>{children}</I18nProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

```tsx
// In any client component (post-migration):
import { useT } from "@/i18n/useT";
export default function MyClient() {
  const { t } = useT();            // ← DESTRUCTURE .t
  return <h1>{t("page.foo.title")}</h1>;
}
```

**Pitfall #41 (SUPERSEDED) — original cookie-on-mount pattern:** the
earlier "useT reads `document.cookie` in useEffect" pattern causes
a brief flash of the default locale on first paint, AND a
hydration mismatch warning in the Next 15 console. The current
context-based pattern below replaces it. Do not use the old
pattern in new code.

**Pitfall #54 — `useT()` returns a context OBJECT, not a function.**
The hook returns `{ locale, dir, meta, t }`. If you write
`const t = useT()` and then call `t("page.foo")`, you get
`TypeError: t is not a function` at first render. Always
destructure: `const { t } = useT();` (or
`const t = useT().t;`). Symptom: page returns 500, dev log shows
`⨯ TypeError: t is not a function at <Component>` with
`digest: "2269236180"`. This pitfall is EASY to miss because in
the context Provider pattern the `t` variable looks callable
when you read the code, but it isn't.

**Pitfall #55 — file extension MUST be `.tsx` for files containing
JSX, even if the file is just a re-export.** `useT.tsx` exists
purely to re-export from `I18nProvider.tsx`, but `.ts` files
cannot have JSX, so the import of `<I18nContext.Provider>` (or
any JSX) breaks the bundler. Symptom:
`Failed to read source code from /src/i18n/useT.ts: No such file
or directory` followed by route 500s — but the file DOES exist
in your IDE. This is the bundler refusing to read a .ts file
whose content is JSX-flavored. Fix: rename `.ts` → `.tsx` and
update all imports. The `tsconfig.json` `allowJs: true` setting
does NOT save you here — JSX in `.ts` is a hard error.

**Pitfall #56 — `t()` takes `vars` as a `Record`, NOT positional
arguments.** The signature is
`t(key: string, vars?: Record<string, string | number>)`. If
you write `t("page.perms_count", n, m)` expecting `{0}` and
`{1}` interpolation (like `printf`), the dict template
`"{count} permissions × {roles} roles"` is returned UNCHANGED
because the second positional arg is ignored and `vars` is
`undefined`. The user sees literal `"{count} permissions ×
{roles} roles"` on the page with no error in the console.
Always pass a record: `t("page.perms_count", { count: n, roles: m })`.
And the dict template uses named placeholders `{count}` not
positional `{0}`.

