## Server component pattern (dashboard layout nav)

```tsx
// src/app/dashboard/layout.tsx
import { getLocale, makeT } from "@/i18n/server";
import LanguageSwitcher from "@/components/LanguageSwitcher";

export default async function DashboardLayout({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  const s = await getSession();
  if (!s?.tenantId) redirect("/login");
  const locale = await getLocale();
  const t = makeT(locale);
  return (
    <div>
      <aside>
        <nav>
          <NavLink href="/dashboard/employees" label={t("nav.employees")} icon="👥" />
          {/* ... */}
        </nav>
      </aside>
      <main>{children}</main>
      <LanguageSwitcher />
    </div>
  );
}
```

**Pitfall #47 — existing dictionaries already have keys you forgot:**
before adding `nav.org` etc., `grep -nE '"nav\.' ar.ts` first.
The HR Bridge session discovered a complete pre-existing `nav.*`
section at lines 9-31 from a prior session, and adding duplicate
keys at line 131+ crashed the layout with `TypeError: makeT is
not a function` (because the optimized bundle of `DICTS_AR`
became invalid). Reuse existing keys (`nav.organization` vs
inventing `nav.org`); only add a new key if it really doesn't
exist.

**Pitfall #48 — login credentials in MEMORY may be stale:** the
HR Bridge memory had `admin@hrbridge.local / admin123` but the
real seed was `naif.sahw@gmail.com / Qweewq123!`. Always grep
`prisma/seed.ts` for `bcrypt.hash` and `email: ` before assuming
login works. Same applies to the session cookie name — the live
iron-session cookie was `hrbridge_session` (HttpOnly), NOT
`hrb_sess` as remembered.

**Pitfall #52 — `DATABASE_URL=file:./dev.db` SILENTLY creates TWO
databases (the silent-failure 401 trap):** this is the #1 reason
`/api/auth/login` returns 401 with the message "بيانات الدخول غير
صحة" even when bcrypt.compare returns `true` and the user exists
in the seeded DB. Mechanism: `file:./dev.db` is resolved by Prisma
from `process.cwd()` (i.e. the directory where `next dev` was
started), NOT from `prisma/schema.prisma`. The `prisma db seed`
command runs from the prisma/ directory and writes to
`prisma/dev.db`. But Next dev starts from the project root and
writes to `<project-root>/dev.db`. SQLite silently creates the
empty file on first open, so you get a fully-functional empty DB
that responds to all queries (just returns no rows). Symptoms:
- `prisma.user.findUnique()` returns `null` for the seeded email
- The seeded user IS visible via `sqlite3 prisma/dev.db`
- `bcrypt.compareSync('Qweewq123!', '<hash-from-prisma/dev.db>')` returns `true` from a node REPL
- `lsof -p <next-dev-pid>` shows the process has BOTH `.db` files open at different times
Fix: in `.env`, set `DATABASE_URL="file:./prisma/dev.db"` (relative
to cwd, which IS the project root). Then `npx prisma generate`
to bake the new path into the generated client, then `pkill -f
"next dev"` + `rm -rf .next` + restart. Verification recipe:
```bash
# After fix, both should show the SAME user count and SAME hash
sqlite3 prisma/dev.db "SELECT count(*), length(password) FROM User"
ls -la /<project-root>/dev.db /<project-root>/prisma/dev.db
# The ROOT dev.db should NOT exist after the fix
# If both exist, Next is still reading the empty one
```
ALSO add `dev.db` AND `prisma/dev.db` to `.gitignore` so a
stray empty DB never gets committed.

**Pitfall #53 — Prisma client caches the DATABASE_URL path at
generate time, not at runtime:** changing `.env` does NOT update
the generated client. Symptom: you fix the URL, restart Next, but
the error is the SAME `Unable to open the database file`. The
generated `@prisma/client` has the OLD path baked in. Fix: ALWAYS
`npx prisma generate` after changing `DATABASE_URL`, BEFORE
restarting the dev server. If you forget this, you'll chase a
phantom config bug for an hour.

