## What this pattern deliberately does NOT do

- **No URL-prefixed locales** (`/ar/login`). Cookies-only is
  simpler for retrofitting; SEO is acceptable since most Arabic
  SaaS doesn't need `/en/` for English-speaking search bots to
  find individual pages.
- **No `[locale]` dynamic segment.** Avoids a massive refactor
  of every existing route.
- **No `next-intl` / `react-i18next` / `@formatjs/intl`.** Zero
  bundle overhead, zero version conflicts.
- **No ICU MessageFormat.** Just `{varName}` interpolation.
  Sufficient for 95% of UI strings. If you need pluralization,
  reach for `Intl.PluralRules` directly.
- **No per-tenant locale override.** All tenants share the
  user's locale. Add `tenant.locale` and prefer it in
  `getLocale()` if you need per-tenant defaults.

---

**Pitfall #57 — `withLocale()` generates locale-prefixed URLs that 404 in
cookie-only locale projects.** The helper `withLocale(locale, path)` in
`src/i18n/index.ts` prepends `/${locale}` to paths (e.g. `/en/dashboard`).
This is correct ONLY if you have `[locale]` dynamic route segments. In the
HR Bridge cookie-locale pattern (no URL prefixes, no middleware rewrites),
`withLocale` produces paths that don't exist — the app 404s after login
because `router.push(withLocale("en", "/dashboard"))` navigates to
`/en/dashboard` which has no matching `app/en/dashboard/page.tsx`.

Symptom: login form submits successfully (API returns `{ok:true}`) but
the browser lands on a 404 page. The URL bar shows `/en/dashboard` or
`/ar/dashboard` instead of `/dashboard`.

Fix: in cookie-locale projects, `withLocale` should strip any existing
locale prefix and return the clean path:
```ts
export function withLocale(_locale: Locale, path: string): string {
  const stripped = path.replace(/^\/(ar|en|fr)(?=\/|$)/, "") || "/";
  return stripped.startsWith("/") ? stripped : "/" + stripped;
}
```
This is a one-line change but it affects login (`router.push(dashboardPath)`)
and register redirects. Always test the full login → dashboard redirect
flow in the browser after touching `withLocale`.

**Pitfall #58 — stale `.next` cache causes "Cannot find module './NNN.js'"
after incremental rebuilds.** When the dev server has been running through
multiple code changes, the `.next` directory accumulates stale chunk
references. Symptom: pages return 200 (Next serves a fallback) but the
dev log shows `⨯ Error: Cannot find module './681.js'` (or similar chunk
numbers) and Fast Refresh does a full reload. This is NOT a source bug —
typecheck and build pass clean. Fix: `pkill -f "next dev"` → `rm -rf .next`
→ restart dev server → hard-refresh browser. This is the same class of
stale-chunk error documented in the nextjs-runtime-errors reference file.

**Pitfall #59 — child client components hardcode Arabic strings instead of
using `t()`.** The parent `page.tsx` server components correctly use
`makeT(locale)` / `t()` for headings and KPI labels, but child client
components (`*Client.tsx`, `EmployeeTable.tsx`, `AddEmployeeButton.tsx`,
etc.) often have Arabic strings hardcoded directly in JSX — table headers,
button labels, search placeholders, toast messages, status badges, form
field labels, select option labels, etc.

Symptom: switching the language from Arabic to English (or French) only
translates the page heading and KPI labels — everything inside the child
component (table, buttons, forms, status badges) stays in Arabic. The
language switcher appears to "not work" for most of the page.

Root cause: when the i18n layer was retrofitted, the parent `page.tsx`
files were updated to use `t()` but the interactive child components
were left with their original Arabic strings. This is a systemic issue
that can affect 20+ files.

Fix pattern (for each affected client component):
1. Add `import { useT } from "@/i18n/useT";` at the top
2. Add `const { t } = useT();` inside the component
3. Replace every hardcoded Arabic string with `t("key")` call
4. Add the new keys to ALL 3 dictionary files (ar.ts, en.ts, fr.ts)
5. For option labels (nationalities, contract types, etc.), use `t()`
   keys for those too — don't leave any Arabic in the JSX
6. For toast/confirm messages with variables, use interpolation:
   `t("employees.sync.toast.all", { count: 5 })` → `"{count} employees synced"`

Detection: `grep -rl '"[ا-ي]' src/app/dashboard` finds all files with
hardcoded Arabic strings in JSX. Filter to `*Client.tsx` and similar
interactive component files.

Scale: this can be a 20+ file fix. Use subagent delegation (delegate_task)
to fix files in parallel batches of 3-5 files per subagent. Each
subagent adds its own dictionary keys — coordinate to avoid duplicate
key conflicts by having each subagent work on distinct feature areas
(employees, leave, billing, etc.).

**Pitfall #60 — `useT.ts` vs `useT.tsx` import path ambiguity.** The
import is `import { useT } from "@/i18n/useT"` (no extension). If both
`useT.ts` and `useT.tsx` exist, the bundler resolution order may pick
the wrong one. Ensure only ONE file exists at `src/i18n/useT.tsx` (it
must be `.tsx` if it contains or re-exports JSX — see pitfall #55).
Delete any stale `useT.ts` to prevent resolution ambiguity.

---

## Debugging: FR shows Arabic text even though dict key exists

### Root cause: stale Next.js module cache

**Symptom:** The FR dictionary has `"ai_agent.base_url": "URL de base"` (confirmed via `grep` and `tsx -e "import..."`), but the browser renders `الرابط الأساسي` (Arabic) for that one label while the rest of the page is French. No error boundary, no "Hide Errors" button.

**Diagnostic chain:**

1. **Understand the fallback:** `DICTS[locale][key] ?? DICTS[DEFAULT_LOCALE][key] ?? key`
   - `DICTS[fr]["ai_agent.base_url"]` = `undefined` (stale module) → falls through to `DICTS[ar]["ai_agent.base_url"]` → "الرابط الأساسي"
   - If the key were missing from ALL three dicts, you'd see the **raw key** `"ai_agent.base_url"` on screen
   - Seeing `الرابط الأساسي` means FR dict lookup failed but AR dict succeeded → the FR module is stale, not missing

2. **Verify the dict file is correct at the filesystem level:**
   ```bash
   npx tsx -e "import { fr } from './src/i18n/dictionaries/fr'; console.log(Object.keys(fr).length, JSON.stringify(fr['ai_agent.base_url']))"
   # Expected: "1082 \"URL de base\""
   ```

3. **Inject a console.log into `makeT()` to see what the runtime returns:**
   ```ts
   // In src/i18n/index.ts, temporarily patch:
   export function makeT(locale: Locale) {
     const dict = getDict(locale);
     if (typeof window !== 'undefined') {
       console.log('[MAKET] locale=' + locale + ' ai_agent.base_url=', dict['ai_agent.base_url']);
     }
     return function t(key: string, vars?: Record<string, string | number>): string {
       // ...
   ```
   Then reload the page and check the browser console. If `console.log` shows `undefined` for the FR locale even though `tsx` found the key, the module cache is stale.

4. **Fix:**
   ```bash
   pkill -f "next dev"
   rm -rf .next
   npx next dev -p 4000
   ```
   Then hard-refresh the browser (Cmd+Shift+R).

**Pitfall #61 — FR (or any non-default locale) shows DEFAULT_LOCALE text for individual labels after adding new keys to the dict file.** This is NOT a source code bug — it's Next's module graph holding a stale version of the FR dict that predates the key addition. The AR dict works because `DEFAULT_LOCALE = "ar"` means AR modules are prioritized in the initial chunk and always fresh; FR/EN modules are lazy-loaded and may reference old chunk IDs after `rm -rf .next` + restart. Fix is always: clean `.next`, restart dev server, hard-refresh browser. Do NOT waste time re-adding the key, changing the value, or checking TypeScript — if `tsx` finds it and the build compiles clean, the problem is the runtime cache.

**Pitfall #62 — cookie must be set with `path=/` BEFORE the first page navigation for SSR to pick it up.** Setting `document.cookie = "hrb_locale=fr"` (no path) scopes the cookie to the current URL path. Subsequent navigation to `/dashboard/settings/ai-agent` won't send the cookie in the request (the browser checks path scope). Always use `document.cookie = "hrb_locale=fr;path=/"`. When testing via browser_console, set the cookie BEFORE navigating (or on the login page before submitting) so the SSR render sees it:

```js
document.cookie = "hrb_locale=fr;path=/";
// THEN navigate or submit login form
```

**Pitfall #63 — the `browser_navigate` snapshot shows localized text immediately after setting the cookie.** If you set the cookie and the snapshot still shows the default locale, the cookie wasn't set correctly (missing `path=/` or wrong syntax). Verify with `document.cookie` output — the cookie value must match one of `"ar"`, `"en"`, `"fr"`.

---

## Comprehensive i18n audit procedure

When asked to verify that all three locales work correctly across the entire app:

### 1. Static audit: cross-reference all `t()` calls against dicts

```bash
# Extract all unique keys from t() calls in source
grep -rhoP "(?<=t\()['\"]([^'\"]+)['\"]" src/app/ src/components/ | sort -u > /tmp/all_keys.txt

# Check each key exists in all three dictionaries
for dict in ar en fr; do
  echo "=== Missing in $dict ==="
  while read -r key; do
    grep -q "\"$key\":" src/i18n/dictionaries/$dict.ts || echo "  $key"
  done < /tmp/all_keys.txt
done
```

### 2. Browser-based visual verification

For each locale, check at least these pages via `browser_navigate` + `browser_snapshot`:

| Page | AR known value | FR known value | EN known value |
|---|---|---|---|
| Dashboard | `عرض الموظفين` | `Voir les employés` | `View employees` |
| AI Agent Settings | `الرابط الأساسي` | `URL de base` | `Base URL` |
| Employees | `الموظفون` (nav) | `Employés` (nav) | `Employees` (nav) |
| AI Chat | `وكيل الذكاء الاصطناعي` | `Agent IA` | `AI Agent` |
| Reports | `التقارير والذكاء` | `Rapports & BI` | `Reports & BI` |

```javascript
// Per-locale test pattern in browser_console:
document.cookie = "hrb_locale=ar;path=/";
// Reload and snapshot — check heading/prominent text for locale-specific value

document.cookie = "hrb_locale=fr;path=/";
// Reload and snapshot — check same element

document.cookie = "hrb_locale=en;path=/";
// Reload and snapshot — check same element
```

Look for these failure signals in the snapshot:
- **Raw key string** (e.g., `nav.employees`) → key missing from ALL three dicts
- **Arabic text on FR page** → key exists in AR dict but missing from FR dict (fallback to DEFAULT_LOCALE)
- **English text on AR/FR page** → hardcoded English string in JSX (not using `t()`) — see pitfall #59
- **"Hide Errors" button or error boundary** → runtime crash in component, often from undefined `t()` value

### 3. Full-page check: all visible StaticText

The accessibility tree snapshot (`browser_snapshot`) lists every `StaticText` and `link` on the page. For a complete audit, check that ALL text nodes under `- main` are translated. Pay special attention to:
- Button labels
- Form placeholders
- Table column headers
- Status badges
- Toast/notification messages
- Empty-state messages

---

## When to graduate to `next-intl`

This pattern is fine until ~200 keys. After that:
- ICU plural/gender selection becomes painful with `t()`
- Date/number formatting needs per-locale customization
- You want type-safe keys (`t("nav.dashboard")` errors on typo)

At that point migrate to `next-intl` with URL-prefixed locales.
The cookie-only approach above will have given you the locale
detection + middleware skeleton already.

