# CSS class debugging in Next.js + Tailwind dashboards

Session: 2026-06-28, HR Bridge SA dashboard styling pass.

## Problem
Dashboard pages had visual glitches: undefined CSS classes (e.g. `glass-card`), broken gradient borders, and popup stacking conflicts. The app built (Next.js doesn't fail on undefined class names) but rendered incorrectly at runtime.

## Technique: find undefined classes

```bash
# 1. Search for class names that LOOK like they should exist
grep -rn "className=.*\bglass-card\b" src/
# → found in InstallPrompt.tsx, PushPermissionPrompt.tsx, offline/page.tsx

# 2. Check if they're defined in globals.css
grep -n "glass-card" src/app/globals.css
# → nothing — class does not exist

# 3. Check if they're defined ANYWHERE
grep -rn "\.glass-card" src/
# → only usages, no definition
```

**Fix:** Replace the undefined class with its intended inline equivalent, or add it to `globals.css`.

In this session, `glass-card` was intended to mean "glass morphism card with colored border". Since `.card` already existed (`@apply glass rounded-2xl`), we just added the border color inline:

```tsx
// Before (broken — class doesn't exist)
<div className="card glass-card p-4 ...">

// After (works — .card provides glass, inline provides border)
<div className="bg-white/78 rounded-2xl p-4 shadow-2xl border-2 border-emerald-200 backdrop-blur-md">
```

## Technique: fix broken gradient-border

The `.gradient-border` class used a pseudo-element with `padding: 1px` to create a gradient stroke. This doesn't render reliably across browsers because `padding` on a pseudo-element with `position: absolute; inset: 0` has no effect (the element has no content box to pad).

**Broken pattern (don't use):**
```css
.gradient-border::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: 0;
  padding: 1px;              /* ← has no effect on empty pseudo-element */
  border-radius: inherit;
  background: linear-gradient(...);
  -webkit-mask: ...;
  mask-composite: exclude;
}
```

**Working pattern:**
```css
.gradient-border {
  position: relative;
  background: white;
  border-radius: 1rem;
  border: 2px solid transparent;
  background-clip: padding-box;
}
.gradient-border::before {
  content: "";
  position: absolute;
  inset: -2px;               /* extend beyond parent border */
  border-radius: 1.15rem;      /* slightly larger to cover border */
  padding: 2px;
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #006C35, #C5A572, #008f47);
  -webkit-mask: linear-gradient(#fff 0 0) content-box,
                linear-gradient(#fff 0 0);
  -webkit-mask-composite: xor;
  mask-composite: exclude;
  pointer-events: none;
  z-index: 1;
}
```

Key insight: the mask uses `content-box` (the inner area) XOR `padding-box` (the full area including padding) to create a hollow stroke. The parent must have `background-clip: padding-box` so its background doesn't draw under the transparent border.

## Technique: popup z-index stacking

Multiple fixed-position popups (install prompt, push permission prompt, notification dropdown) can block each other if they share the same `z-index` or if one overlaps another's click target.

**Symptom:** Clicking "dismiss" on one popup does nothing because another popup with `z-50` is rendering on top of it, intercepting clicks.

**Fix:** Assign a clear stacking order:
- Notification dropdown (highest, user-initiated): `z-50`
- Push permission prompt (medium, auto-shown): `z-40`  
- Install prompt (lowest, auto-shown): `z-40` but positioned differently

Also add `onClick={(e) => e.stopPropagation()}` to the popup container so clicks on the popup don't bubble to a parent overlay that might have its own dismiss handler.

## Verification after CSS fixes

```bash
# 1. TypeScript still compiles (class names don't affect types)
npx tsc --noEmit

# 2. Build still succeeds
npm run build

# 3. Visual verification — screenshot each affected page
# (use browser_vision or manual browser inspection)
```

**Important:** CSS class errors do NOT fail Next.js builds. You must visually verify or grep for undefined classes separately.

## Commands for CSS class auditing

```bash
# Find all class names that contain "glass" or "card"
grep -ohE 'className="[^"]*"' src/ -r | grep -oE '\b[a-z]+-[a-z]+\b' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -30

# Find classes used in JSX that are NOT in globals.css
cat src/app/globals.css | grep -oE '\.[a-z-]+' | sort -u > /tmp/defined.txt
grep -orhE 'className="[^"]*"' src/ | grep -oE '[a-z]+-[a-z]+' | sort -u > /tmp/used.txt
comm -23 /tmp/used.txt /tmp/defined.txt | head -20
# → shows likely-undefined classes (some may be Tailwind utilities)
```

