# Phase 11.2 — Client `useT()` SSR hydration mismatch fix

**Date discovered:** 2026-06-28
**Project:** HR Bridge SA (Next.js 15 + custom i18n, no third-party libs)
**Status:** Fix applied. /login and /register render correctly in AR/EN/FR on first paint.

## Symptom
`/login` and `/register` show Arabic h1 in all 3 locales on first paint (verified via curl with `hrb_locale=en` cookie). `<html lang dir>` flips correctly (`lang="en" dir="ltr"`), but inline strings stay Arabic.

After client hydration, the strings update to the right language (visible as a flash of Arabic then English/French).

## Root cause
The original `src/i18n/useT.ts` was:
```ts
"use client";
import { useMemo } from "react";
import { makeT, DEFAULT_LOCALE, ... } from "./index";

function readLocaleFromCookie(): Locale {
  if (typeof document === "undefined") return DEFAULT_LOCALE;
  const m = document.cookie.match(/(?:^|;\s*)hrb_locale=([^;]+)/);
  ...
}

export function useT() {
  const locale = useMemo(() => readLocaleFromCookie(), []);
  const t = useMemo(() => makeT(locale), [locale]);
  return { t, locale, dir: dirFor(locale), meta: LOCALE_META[locale] };
}
```

During server-side render, `document` is undefined → returns `DEFAULT_LOCALE` (`"ar"`). The HTML is rendered with Arabic strings. Then on client mount, `document.cookie` is read → strings update.

## Why server-side `<html lang dir>` worked
Root layout (`src/app/layout.tsx`) is a server component that calls `await getLocale()` which uses `cookies()` from `next/headers`. Server-side cookies work fine — that's why `<html lang="en" dir="ltr">` rendered correctly. The mismatch was ONLY in client components reading `document.cookie`.

## Fix — React Context provider

### Step 1: Create `src/i18n/I18nProvider.tsx`
```tsx
"use client";
import { createContext, useContext, useMemo, ReactNode } from "react";
import {
  DEFAULT_LOCALE,
  dirFor,
  LOCALE_META,
  makeT,
  type Locale,
} from "./index";

type I18nContextValue = {
  locale: Locale;
  dir: "ltr" | "rtl";
  meta: (typeof LOCALE_META)[Locale];
  t: (key: string, vars?: Record<string, string | number>) => string;
};

const I18nContext = createContext<I18nContextValue | null>(null);

export function I18nProvider({
  locale,
  children,
}: {
  locale: Locale;
  children: ReactNode;
}) {
  const value = useMemo<I18nContextValue>(() => {
    const t = makeT(locale);
    return {
      locale,
      dir: dirFor(locale),
      meta: LOCALE_META[locale],
      t,
    };
  }, [locale]);
  return <I18nContext.Provider value={value}>{children}</I18nContext.Provider>;
}

export function useT(): I18nContextValue {
  const ctx = useContext(I18nContext);
  // Fallback for error-boundary renders outside the provider
  if (!ctx) {
    const t = makeT(DEFAULT_LOCALE);
    return {
      locale: DEFAULT_LOCALE,
      dir: dirFor(DEFAULT_LOCALE),
      meta: LOCALE_META[DEFAULT_LOCALE],
      t,
    };
  }
  return ctx;
}

export function useLocale(): Locale {
  return useT().locale;
}

export function useDir(): "ltr" | "rtl" {
  return useT().dir;
}
```

### Step 2: Make `src/i18n/useT.tsx` a thin re-export
The original `useT.ts` had `"use client"` directive but was `.ts`. When you change it to re-export `I18nProvider` (which uses JSX), the file MUST be `.tsx` or TS lint complains about `'--jsx' is not set`.

```tsx
// src/i18n/useT.tsx
export { useT, useLocale, useDir, I18nProvider } from "./I18nProvider";
```

If the file was previously `.ts`, rename:
```bash
mv src/i18n/useT.ts src/i18n/useT.tsx
```
All imports `from "@/i18n/useT"` still work (TS resolves `.tsx` automatically).

### Step 3: Wrap root layout with the provider
```tsx
// src/app/layout.tsx
import { I18nProvider } from "@/i18n/I18nProvider";

export default async function RootLayout({ children }) {
  const locale = await getLocale();
  const dir = dirFor(locale);
  return (
    <html lang={locale} dir={dir}>
      <body>
        <PWAInit />
        <OfflineBadge />
        ...
        <I18nProvider locale={locale}>{children}</I18nProvider>
      </body>
    </html>
  );
}
```

### Step 4: (Consumer-side change — destructure `t`)
`useT()` returns an `I18nContextValue` object: `{ locale, dir, meta, t }`. You MUST destructure:
```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT
const { t } = useT();
return <h1>{t("page.dashboard.title")}</h1>;

// ❌ WRONG — `t` becomes the whole object, calling t(key) → "t is not a function"
const t = useT();
return <h1>{t("page.dashboard.title")}</h1>;
```

Or use the shorthand `const t = useT().t;` if you only need `t`.
This bites HARD on first SSR render — the page goes 500 with "t is not a function".

## Verification
```bash
# Restart and curl
curl -s http://localhost:4000/login -H 'Cookie: hrb_locale=en' | grep -oE '<h1[^>]*>[^<]+</h1>'
# Expected: <h1 ...>Sign in</h1>

curl -s http://localhost:4000/login -H 'Cookie: hrb_locale=fr' | grep -oE '<h1[^>]*>[^<]+</h1>'
# Expected: <h1 ...>Connexion</h1>

curl -s http://localhost:4000/register -H 'Cookie: hrb_locale=ar' | grep -oE '<h1[^>]*>[^<]+</h1>'
# Expected: <h1 ...>تسجيل منشأة جديدة</h1>
```

## Why this is a CLASS-LEVEL pitfall
ANY Next.js i18n implementation where:
- Server-side reads cookie via `getLocale()` for `<html lang dir>`
- Client-side reads cookie via `document.cookie` for inline strings

will have this hydration mismatch. The fix (context provider from server-rendered locale) is universal. Alternative fixes:
- Use `next-intl` or similar (full-stack i18n lib that handles this)
- Use `getLocale()` in every page (forces server components — blocks `"use client"` usage)
- Use I18nProvider as described here (keeps `useT` API stable, minimal refactor)

## Related pitfalls
- Phase 11.1 — Prisma `file:` path → `references/phase-11-prisma-db-path.md`
- Phase 11.3 — Duplicate dictionary keys (in SKILL.md body)
- Phase 11.4 — Triage order for "wrong locale" (in SKILL.md body)
- Phase 10d — Common i18n traps (components outside provider, stale cache, module-level constants) → `references/i18n-common-traps.md`

## Common layout bug — components outside I18nProvider

**Symptom:** `InstallPrompt`/`OfflineBadge`/`PushPermissionPrompt` show Arabic on EN/FR pages while the rest of the page renders correctly. Locale switch via cookie has no effect.

**Root cause:** In `layout.tsx`, these components were placed BEFORE `<I18nProvider>`, so `useT()` context fallback returns `DEFAULT_LOCALE` (Arabic in Arabic-first SaaS).

**Fix:** Move ALL `useT()` client components inside the provider:
```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT
<I18nProvider locale={locale}>
  <OfflineBadge />
  <PushPermissionPrompt />
  <InstallPrompt />
  {children}
</I18nProvider>
```

**Verification:** After fixing, `document.cookie = "hrb_locale=en;path=/"; location.reload()` must show English text in InstallPrompt, not "تثبيت الآن" / "لاحقاً".

references.references/white-label-agency.md:
# White-label / agency / reseller tier (Q.af Phase 7 reference)

Full recipe for shipping the "agency" tier of a multi-tenant SaaS: each
agency owns its branding (logo, color, custom domain, email footer) and
can mint sub-tenants that inherit the agency's look. Built and verified
on Q.af.

## Why a dedicated phase

Once a SaaS has tenants, the next revenue question is "can I resell this
to my clients under my own brand?" White-label (or "agency") mode answers
that: an agency owns N sub-tenants, each sub-tenant gets the agency's
branding on every email + dashboard touch-point, and the agency is the
billing entity for the whole constellation.

The shape is small (one extra model + a few routes + one email template)
but the integration points are everywhere — signup, password reset,
invoice notifications, the admin module — so the payoff of a dedicated
phase is keeping the agency code from sprawling into every existing route.

## 1. Data model — one new model + one FK

```prisma
// Add agencyId to Tenant:
model Tenant {
  // ...existing fields...
  agencyId  String?
  agency    Agency?  @relation("AgencyTenants", fields: [agencyId], references: [id])
  // ...existing relations...

  @@index([agencyId])
}

// New Agency model:
model Agency {
  id            String   @id @default(cuid())
  slug          String   @unique             // /agency/<slug>, also used as email "from" name
  name          String                       // display name (Arabic or Latin)
  logoUrl       String?                      // absolute URL; shown in dashboard + emails
  brandColor    String   @default("#7c3aed") // primary brand color
  emailFooter   String?                      // custom Arabic footer for transactional email
  customDomain  String?  @unique             // e.g. links.agency.com — verified separately
  webhookUrl    String?                      // optional outbound webhook for billing events
  billingEmail  String?                      // invoice notifications
  active        Boolean  @default(true)
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now())

  tenants  Tenant[] @relation("AgencyTenants")

  @@index([slug])
}
```

Migration: `npx prisma migrate dev --name add_agency_white_label --create-only`,
edit if needed, then `npx prisma migrate deploy`. Regenerate the client
BEFORE any TypeScript work that touches `agencyId` (see pitfall #7 in
the main skill — stale Prisma client yields "Property 'agency' does not
exist on type 'Tenant'").

### Why agencyId on Tenant, not TenantId on Agency
- An agency can hold many sub-tenants (`Tenant.agencyId` is the FK).
- The relation self-naming (`AgencyTenants`) is required because both
  models reference each other; Prisma needs a name to disambiguate.
- `customDomain` is `@unique` because two agencies can't both own the
  same branded domain. If you skip `@unique`, two agencies could mint
  `links.agency.com` independently and the resolver can't pick one.

## 2. Super-admin agency CRUD

### `GET /api/admin/agencies` — list agencies
Auth via `requireAdmin()` (which now uses `{ allowAdmin: true }` — see
pitfall #13 in the main skill). Returns the agencies with `_count.tenants`
so the UI can show "5 clients" badges without a second query.

### `POST /api/admin/agencies` — create
Body shape:
```ts
{
  name: string,           // required
  slug?: string,          // auto-derived from name via slugify() if missing
  logoUrl?: string,
  brandColor?: string,    // hex, defaults to #7c3aed
  customDomain?: string,  // lowercased
  emailFooter?: string,
  billingEmail?: string,  // lowercased
  webhookUrl?: string,
  active?: boolean,       // defaults to true
}
```

Slug uniqueness handling — collisions happen (two agencies named "Acme
Marketing") so loop with a numeric suffix:
```ts
let suffix = 0;
let final = slug;
while (await prisma.agency.findUnique({ where: { slug: final } })) {
  suffix++;
  final = `${slug}-${suffix}`;
}
```

`customDomain` needs the same treatment — it's `@unique` per the schema
above. The seed creates `links.demo-agency.test`; a re-run of the same
e2e POSTing `links.test.example` won't collide, but a POST with the same
seed value will. Disambiguate by suffixing the host segment:
```ts
let domain = body.customDomain?.toLowerCase().trim() ?? null;
if (domain) {
  let dSuf = 0, dFinal = domain;
  while (await prisma.agency.findUnique({ where: { customDomain: dFinal } })) {
    dSuf++;
    const dot = domain.indexOf(".");
    if (dot < 0) { dFinal = `${domain}-${dSuf}`; break; }
    dFinal = `${domain.slice(0, dot)}-${dSuf}${domain.slice(dot)}`;
    if (dSuf > 50) break;  // safety valve
  }
  domain = dFinal;
}
```

And wrap `prisma.agency.create` in a `try/catch` for P2002 as a
race-condition net:
```ts
try {
  return NextResponse.json({ agency: await prisma.agency.create({...}) });
} catch (e: any) {
  if (e?.code === "P2002") {
    return NextResponse.json(
      { error: "duplicate", field: e?.meta?.target?.[0] ?? null },
      { status: 409 }
    );
  }
  throw e;
}
```

Without this, running the same e2e twice in a row returns 500 instead
of 409. See pitfall #20 in the main skill for the general pattern.

The `slugify()` helper must accept Arabic too (preserve the Arabic block):
```ts
function slugify(s: string): string {
  return s.toLowerCase().trim()
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9\u0600-\u06FF-]+/g, "-")
    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, "")
    .slice(0, 48);
}
```

### `POST /api/admin/agencies/[id]/tenants` — mint sub-tenant
Body: `{ name, type?, plan? }`. Returns the new tenant AND the parent
agency's brand payload (`{ id, slug, name, brandColor }`) so the dashboard
can immediately apply the agency's color to the new tenant's view.

A super-admin uses this to "open a workspace" for a client under an
agency — it's the white-label equivalent of manual signup but with the
`agencyId` populated from the start.

## 3. Brand resolver + email template

`src/lib/agency.ts`:

```ts
export type AgencyBrand = {
  name: string;
  slug: string;
  logoUrl: string | null;
  brandColor: string;
  emailFooter: string | null;
};

export async function resolveAgency(tenantId: string): Promise<AgencyBrand | null> {
  const t = await prisma.tenant.findUnique({
    where: { id: tenantId },
    select: { agency: true },
  });
  if (!t?.agency) return null;
  return {
    name: t.agency.name,
    slug: t.agency.slug,
    logoUrl: t.agency.logoUrl,
    brandColor: t.agency.brandColor,
    emailFooter: t.agency.emailFooter,
  };
}

export function renderBrandedEmail(opts: {
  brand: AgencyBrand | null;
  subject: string;
  bodyHtml: string;
  lang?: "ar" | "en";
}): { html: string; subject: string } {
  const color = opts.brand?.brandColor ?? "#7c3aed";
  const logoBlock = opts.brand?.logoUrl
    ? `<img src="${opts.brand.logoUrl}" alt="${opts.brand.name}" style="height:48px;margin-bottom:12px"/>`
    : `<div style="height:48px;width:48px;border-radius:12px;background:${color};…">${(opts.brand?.name ?? "Q.af")[0]}</div>`;
  const footer = opts.brand?.emailFooter ?? `أُرسلت من ${opts.brand?.name ?? "Q.af"}`;
  const dir = (opts.lang ?? "ar") === "ar" ? "rtl" : "ltr";
  const html = `<!doctype html><html lang="${opts.lang ?? "ar"}" dir="${dir}"><body style="…"><table …><tr><td …>${logoBlock}<h1>${opts.brand?.name ?? "Q.af"}</h1></td></tr><tr><td …>${opts.bodyHtml}</td></tr><tr><td …>${footer}</td></tr></table></body></html>`;
  return { html, subject: opts.subject };
}
```

Wire it into signup + invoice flows:

```ts
// /api/auth/signup, after creating the tenant:
const brand = await resolveAgency(tenant.id);
if (process.env.SMTP_URL) {
  const { subject, html } = renderBrandedEmail({
    brand,
    subject: "مرحباً بك",
    bodyHtml: `<p>مرحباً ${name}،</p><p>مساحة عملك جاهزة: <strong>${tenant.name}</strong></p>`,
  });
  await sendMail({ to: email, subject, html });
}

// /api/payments/webhook/[gateway], after marking payment paid:
const brand = await resolveAgency(payment.tenantId);
const invoice = await renderZatcaInvoice(payment);
if (process.env.SMTP_URL) {
  const { subject, html } = renderBrandedEmail({
    brand,
    subject: `فاتورة ${invoice.invoiceNo}`,
    bodyHtml: `<p>فاتورتك جاهزة:</p><p>${invoice.invoiceNo} — ${invoice.total} ${invoice.currency}</p>`,
  });
  await sendMail({ to: payment.billingEmail, subject, html });
}
```

If `process.env.SMTP_URL` is not set, `sendMail` becomes a no-op and the
brand still renders correctly in the on-screen invoice. Don't ship an
SMTP integration in this phase — just thread the brand through so it's
ready when email lands.

## 4. Public agency landing page — `/agency/[slug]/page.tsx`

Public. No auth. Resolves the agency + its tenants and renders a branded
showcase. Renders as a server component (no JS required for the visible
content). Sections:

```
[logo or initial 64×64] [agency name h1] [customDomain if set]
[intro paragraph: agency pitch, Arabic]
[badge: "وكالة معتمدة على Q.af"]
[tenants grid: card per tenant (name + plan)]
[footer: agency emailFooter or default © year agency]
[floating chip: "Q.af — منصة الروابط القصيرة"]
```

Style: light, RTL, `dir="rtl"` on the root, `background: radial-gradient
with brandColor at 0% 0%`. The chip in the bottom-right is a `<Link
href="/">` that brings curious visitors to Q.af itself — turn the
landing page into a soft funnel back to the platform.

Gotcha: `notFound()` from `next/navigation` if agency doesn't exist OR
if `agency.active === false` — never 404 silently, just hide it.

## 5. Admin UI — `/admin/agencies`

Server-component page that delegates to `AgenciesClient` (a `"use client"`
component because it has forms + modals):

```tsx
// /admin/agencies/page.tsx (server)
import { requireAdmin } from "@/lib/adminGuard";
import { redirect } from "next/navigation";
import AgenciesClient from "./AgenciesClient";

export const dynamic = "force-dynamic";

export default async function AdminAgenciesPage() {
  const guard = await requireAdmin();
  if (!guard.ok) redirect("/login?next=/admin/agencies");
  return <AgenciesClient initial={guard.auth} />;
}
```

The client renders:
- Top bar: title + "+ وكالة جديدة" button that toggles a create form.
- Create form: name + logoUrl + brandColor (with a palette of 5
  pre-defined colors + a free `<input type="color">`) + customDomain +
  billingEmail + emailFooter.
- Card grid: one card per agency showing initial/logo (with `brandColor`
  background fallback), name, slug.qaf.link, tenantCount, status pill.
- Per-card actions: "+ عميل جديد" (opens a modal to mint a sub-tenant)
  and "صفحة عامة" (links to /agency/<slug>).

Modal: a single `<div className="fixed inset-0 bg-black/30 …"
onClick={() => setMinting(null)}>` with `e.stopPropagation()` on the
inner panel. Don't pull in a modal library for one form.

Add `agencies` to the `NAV` array in `src/components/AdminShell.tsx`:

```ts
const NAV = [
  { href: "/admin",            labelAr: "نظرة عامة",  key: "overview" },
  // …existing entries…
  { href: "/admin/agencies",   labelAr: "الوكالات",    key: "agencies" },
];
```

## 6. Verification ladder

1. `npx tsc --noEmit` clean (with `Agency` + `Tenant.agencyId` migrated).
2. `curl -b admin-cookies.txt http://localhost:3000/api/admin/agencies` →
   `200`, JSON with `agencies: []`.
3. `curl -b admin-cookies.txt -X POST …/api/admin/agencies -d '{"name":"Acme"}'`
   → `200` with `{ agency: { id, slug: "acme", … } }`.
4. `curl -b admin-cookies.txt -X POST …/api/admin/agencies/<id>/tenants -d '{"name":"Client One"}'`
   → `200` with `{ tenant: { id, agencyId, … } }`.
5. `curl http://localhost:3000/agency/acme` → `200`, HTML contains the
   agency name + at least one tenant card.
6. `/admin/agencies` renders the new agency as a card; clicking
   "+ عميل جديد" opens the modal; submitting creates a tenant that
   appears on the public agency page.
7. Re-run the existing analytics + Phase 5 e2e harnesses to confirm no
   regressions (the agency module is additive — shouldn't break anything).

## 7. Common extension points

- **Custom domain DNS verification for agencies** — reuse the Phase 3
  `lib/domain-verify.ts` + `Domain` table. Add a `domainType` discriminator
  ("agency" vs "workspace") and the `/agency/[slug]` route checks if the
  request host matches a verified agency custom domain before resolving
  by slug. Same `verifyStatus` + `dnsTarget` + `failCount` machinery.
- **Agency billing rollup** — `/api/admin/agencies/[id]/billing` sums
  paid payments across all sub-tenants for the agency. Useful for
  monthly invoicing the agency itself.
- **Per-agency sub-domain vending** — when an agency mints a tenant, also
  vend `<tenant>.agency.customDomain` automatically. Reuse the free
  subdomain vending from Phase 1 with the agency's `customDomain` as
  the suffix instead of `qaf.link`.
- **Agency-branded ZATCA invoice PDF** — extend the Phase 5 ZATCA XML
  generator with an optional `agency` block so the printed invoice shows
  the agency's logo, color, and footer in addition to the workspace's.
  End customers see one consistent brand throughout.
- **Agency API key** — `Agency.apiKeyHash` (sha256 of a random token) so
  the agency can mint tenants programmatically via `POST /api/v1/agency/
  tenants` with `Authorization: Bearer <key>`. Bypass `requireAdmin` for
  agency-scoped routes by checking the key against the agency's hash.

references.references/zatca-vat-cr-management.md:
# Phase 9.2 — VAT/CR field management behind the ZATCA invoice

Phase 9.2 ships the *source of truth* — the VAT and Commercial Registration (السجل
التجاري) numbers that get stamped into the XML. Without these fields the invoice
download shows `<cbc:ID schemeID="VAT"></cbc:ID>` — an instant ZATCA reject.

> **ZATCA XML element shape (verified against the running Phase 9.2 code)**: the
> seller's VAT is stamped inside `<cac:AccountingSupplierParty>/<cac:Party>` as
> `<cbc:ID schemeID="VAT">310000000000004</cbc:ID>`. The older
> `<>` form is NOT what this code emits — don't grep for it.

This recipe covers the schema, both PATCH routes, both settings UIs, the
sellerVat resolution chain, and the seed backfill. Verified end-to-end against
Q.af Phase 9.2 in this session: `tsc --noEmit` exit 0, `npm run build` exit 0,
two e2e shells (e2e-bio, e2e-phase5) re-run green after the changes.

## 1. Schema (run once)

Append nullable VAT + CR to both `Tenant` and `Agency`:

```prisma
model Tenant {
  // ...existing fields...
  vatNumber String?   // 15 digits
  crNumber  String?   // 10 digits
}

model Agency {
  // ...existing fields...
  vatNumber String?
  crNumber  String?
}
```

Migrate + regenerate + restart:

```bash
npx prisma migrate dev --name add_vat_cr_numbers --skip-seed
npx prisma generate
# kill the dev server, restart — hot-reload does not reload @prisma/client
pkill -f "next dev" ; sleep 2
cd app && nohup npx next dev -H 0.0.0.0 -p 3000 > /tmp/qaf-dev.log 2>&1 &
sleep 6 && curl -s -o /dev/null -w "%{http_code}\n" http://127.0.0.1:3000/
```

If you skip the restart, the next request to a route that reads the new field
returns 500 with `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'vatNumber')` —
`tsc` is happy because the on-disk client is correct, but the running Node
process holds the old client in memory.

## 2. sellerVat resolution chain (the only correct order)

Invoices must show the **seller**'s VAT, which in a white-label setup is the
**agency**'s VAT, not the sub-tenant's. Always:

```ts
const sellerVat = payment.tenant?.agency?.vatNumber
              || payment.tenant?.vatNumber
              || "";
```

Apply in BOTH routes:
- `src/app/api/invoices/[id]/xml/route.ts` (tenant-facing download)
- `src/app/api/admin/invoices/[id]/xml/route.ts` (super-admin download)

The admin route must explicitly extend the Prisma `select`:

```ts
const payment = await prisma.payment.findUnique({
  where: { id },
  include: {
    tenant: {
      select: {
        vatNumber: true,
        crNumber: true,
        agency: { select: { name: true, vatNumber: true, crNumber: true } },
      },
    },
  },
});
```

If the admin select omits `vatNumber` on the agency, the override is invisible —
the admin download shows the sub-tenant's VAT when the agency's was intended.
This bit this session: the admin route was using the default `include: { tenant: true }`
and only the tenant-level `vatNumber` was reaching the XML.

## 3. PATCH routes

### Tenant PATCH — `src/app/api/tenants/[id]/route.ts`

Accept `body.vat` and `body.cr` on the existing PATCH handler:

```ts
function cleanVat(s: string | undefined): string | null {
  if (s === undefined) return undefined as any;  // "not present" — leave alone
  const digits = String(s).replace(/\D/g, "");
  if (digits === "") return null;                  // "" → clear
  return digits.slice(0, 15);
}
function cleanCr(s: string | undefined): string | null {
  if (s === undefined) return undefined as any;
  const digits = String(s).replace(/\D/g, "");
  if (digits === "") return null;
  return digits.slice(0, 10);
}

// inside the PATCH handler, after authz:
if (body.vat !== undefined) data.vatNumber = cleanVat(body.vat);
if (body.cr  !== undefined) data.crNumber  = cleanCr(body.cr);
```

Returning `undefined` from the cleaner signals "field not present in the
request — leave existing value alone". Returning `null` signals "explicitly
clear". Distinguishing these is the difference between a PATCH that nukes a
field you didn't touch and one that respects the partial-update contract.

### Agency PATCH — `src/app/api/agencies/[id]/route.ts` (new file)

```ts
import { NextRequest, NextResponse } from "next/server";
import { prisma } from "@/lib/prisma";
import { getAuth } from "@/lib/auth";

export async function PATCH(
  req: NextRequest,
  { params }: { params: Promise<{ id: string }> }
) {
  const { id } = await params;          // Next 16: params is a Promise
  const auth = await getAuth();
  if (!auth?.user) return NextResponse.json({ error: "unauth" }, { status: 401 });

  // caller must be OWNER/ADMIN of a sub-tenant that belongs to this agency
  const callerTenant = await prisma.tenant.findFirst({
    where: { id: auth.tenantId ?? "__none__" },
    select: { agencyId: true },
  });
  if (callerTenant?.agencyId !== id) {
    return NextResponse.json({ error: "forbidden" }, { status: 403 });
  }
  // optionally: also require role === "OWNER" || role === "ADMIN"

  const body = await req.json();
  const data: any = {};
  if (typeof body.name === "string")         data.name = body.name.trim().slice(0, 80);
  if (typeof body.logoUrl === "string")      data.logoUrl = body.logoUrl;
  if (typeof body.primaryColor === "string") data.primaryColor = body.primaryColor;
  if (body.vat !== undefined)                data.vatNumber = cleanVat(body.vat);
  if (body.cr  !== undefined)                data.crNumber  = cleanCr(body.cr);

  const agency = await prisma.agency.update({ where: { id }, data });
  return NextResponse.json({ agency });
}
```

Also extend the existing `GET /api/agencies/[id]/billing/route.ts` to include
the two new fields in its response so the dashboard edit form can pre-fill.

## 4. Settings UI — tenant side

`src/app/dashboard/settings/SettingsClient.tsx` (or its successor). Add VAT + CR
inputs to the existing settings form:

```tsx
const [vat, setVat] = useState(tenant.vatNumber ?? "");
const [cr,  setCr]  = useState(tenant.crNumber  ?? "");
const [msg, setMsg] = useState<{ ok: boolean; text: string } | null>(null);

async function saveZatca() {
  setMsg(null);
  const r = await fetch(`/api/tenants/${tenant.id}`, {
    method: "PATCH",
    headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
    body: JSON.stringify({ vat, cr }),
  });
  const j = await r.json();
  if (!r.ok) { setMsg({ ok: false, text: j.error ?? "فشل الحفظ" }); return; }
  setMsg({ ok: true, text: "تم الحفظ" });
  router.refresh();
}

<label>الرقم الضريبي (15 رقم)</label>
<input
  value={vat}
  onChange={(e) => setVat(e.target.value.replace(/\D/g, "").slice(0, 15))}
  inputMode="numeric"
  className="ltr:text-left"
  dir="ltr"
/>
<label>السجل التجاري (10 أرقام)</label>
<input ... />
<button onClick={saveZatca}>حفظ</button>
{msg && <p className={msg.ok ? "text-emerald-600" : "text-rose-600"}>{msg.text}</p>}
```

Two RTL details that bit this session: the input must be `dir="ltr"` (digits
and the `\d` keypad flow right-to-left in RTL containers otherwise) AND the
input wrapper text class needs `ltr:text-left` to anchor the cursor. The
class is the lighter fix; `dir="ltr"` on the input is the binding one.

## 5. Settings UI — agency side

`src/app/dashboard/agency/page.tsx`. Same edit card, inside the agency settings
panel, with a `useRouter().refresh()` after the PATCH. The agency's billing
endpoint already returns the fields, so the form pre-fills automatically.

The agency-side card and the tenant-side card are intentionally identical
(visual + copy). The only difference is the URL the form PATCHes —
`/api/tenants/<tenantId>` vs `/api/agencies/<agencyId>`. Resist the urge to
share a component across them — the auth + ownership rules differ enough that
two near-duplicate components are clearer than a heavily-parameterized one.

## 6. Seed backfill

In `scripts/seed-phase5.ts` (or whichever seed creates the demo tenant + demo
agency), add the two fields. Wrap the new block inside the existing
`main().catch().finally(async () => { …; await prisma.$disconnect(); })` —
top-level await in CJS seed scripts is illegal (tsx transform rejects it).

Realistic ZATCA-format demo values (these are valid format-wise, not registered):

| Entity           | VAT               | CR         |
|------------------|-------------------|------------|
| demo tenant      | `300000000000003` | `1010101010` |
| demo agency      | `310000000000004` | `2020202020` |

```ts
// inside main()
const demoTenant = await prisma.tenant.upsert({
  where: { id: DEMO_TENANT_ID },
  update: { vatNumber: "300000000000003", crNumber: "1010101010" },
  create: { /* ... existing fields ..., */ vatNumber: "300000000000003", crNumber: "1010101010" },
});
// same for the agency — both update AND create branches get the values
```

## 7. Verification ladder

```bash
JAR=$(mktemp)
curl -s -c "$JAR" -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/auth/login \
  -H "content-type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email":"demo@qaf.test","password":"<seeded>"}' >/dev/null

# 1) pick a paid payment id
PID=$(curl -s -b "$JAR" "http://127.0.0.1:3000/dashboard/payments" \
  | grep -oE 'dashboard/invoices/[a-z0-9-]+' | head -1 | cut -d/ -f3)

# 2) invoice XML now contains the seeded VAT
curl -s -b "$JAR" "http://127.0.0.1:3000/dashboard/invoices/$PID/xml" -o /tmp/inv.xml
xmllint --noout /tmp/inv.xml && echo XML_VALID
grep -E "<cbc:ID schemeID="VAT">(300000000000003|310000000000004)</cbc:ID>" /tmp/inv.xml

# 3) PATCH VAT and confirm it appears in the next XML
TID=<tenant-id>
curl -s -b "$JAR" -X PATCH "http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/tenants/$TID" \
  -H "content-type: application/json" -d '{"vat":"300999999999993"}' | head
curl -s -b "$JAR" "http://127.0.0.1:3000/dashboard/invoices/$PID/xml" \
  | grep -E "<cbc:ID schemeID="VAT">300999999999993</cbc:ID>"

# 4) clear + confirm
curl -s -b "$JAR" -X PATCH "http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/tenants/$TID" \
  -H "content-type: application/json" -d '{"vat":""}'
curl -s -b "$JAR" "http://127.0.0.1:3000/dashboard/invoices/$PID/xml" \
  | grep -E '<cbc:ID schemeID="VAT"></cbc:ID>'   # expect: matches (empty)
```

If step 2 returns no match, the sellerVat chain is broken in the wrong place:
- chain returns "" for a tenant with `vatNumber` set → the `select` on the
  Prisma query is omitting the field (common in the admin route).
- chain returns "" for a tenant that DOES have the field set → `payment.tenant`
  is null because the route used `findUnique({ where: { id } })` without
  `include: { tenant: true }`.

## 8. Common mistakes (not all were hit this session — some are next-phase hazards)

- **Editing `prisma/schema.prisma` but forgetting `prisma generate`**. The new
  field is in the DB but `tenant.vatNumber` is `undefined` at runtime. Run
  `npx prisma generate` after every `migrate dev`.
- **Editing the XML route but not the admin XML route** (or vice-versa). The
  two routes are independent code paths; both need the chain.
- **Adding `dir="rtl"` to the VAT input** — digits flow right-to-left and the
  cursor jumps on every keystroke. Force `dir="ltr"` on digit-only inputs.
- **Server-action form for the settings** — using `<form action={...}>` loses
  the inline error UX and the show-once secret pattern. Stick with
  `useState + fetch + router.refresh()` per dashboard CRUD pattern.
- **Defaulting to `body.vat = ""` on update** — the PATCH cleaner must
  distinguish "field not present" (keep existing) from "field set to empty"
  (clear to null). One undefined-check is enough; do not assume missing == empty.

## 9. What this phase does NOT cover (out of scope, prod-only)

- **ZATCA cryptographic stamp** (CSR, CSID, signing the XML with the device
  certificate). That requires registering with the Fatoora platform and is
  a separate Phase 10+ integration. The XML this phase emits is well-formed
  UBL 2.1 and download-ready, but it does not yet carry the `ext:UBLExtensions`
  signature block.
- **QR code on the invoice page** — ZATCA Phase 2 also requires a TLV-encoded
  QR. Trivial to add via a `qrcode` npm package, but kept out of this phase
  to keep the diff focused on the source-of-truth fields.
- **Multi-language invoice copy** (Arabic + English on the same invoice). The
  current invoice is Arabic-only; bilingual is a Phase 10+ requirement.

